From: rumisle <why-excuse-quail@duck.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:04:41 +0800
Subject: web/request-queue: writes go first; more parallel reads over HTTP/2

The app sends at most 4 API requests at a time, first come first served, to
stay inside HTTP/1.1's six connections per origin. After a reconnect it
re-syncs every directory it holds (about ten catalog reads each), so on a slow
link a prompt sent then waited behind ~200 reads: about a minute before the
server even saw it.

Anything that is not a GET or HEAD (sending a prompt, answering a permission,
stopping a run) now goes ahead of the waiting reads, like transcript reads
already do. When the page itself came over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, requests to its
origin share one connection, so the limit there is 16.

Ocelot-Patch: web/request-priority

